---
const costs = [
  {
    front: '$2k-$15k / month',
    label: 'Support-heavy flow',
    back: 'Support thinks it is a ticket problem. Product thinks it is friction. The missing scaffold keeps moving the cost across teams.',
  },
  {
    front: '$10k-$75k / month',
    label: 'Repeated onboarding drag',
    back: 'Implementation explains what the product should have sequenced. Activation slows while the same gap is re-taught.',
  },
  {
    front: 'Bad data',
    label: 'Weak intake scaffold',
    back: 'The team debates lead quality after the form already invited the wrong answer. Qualification absorbs the missing guidance.',
  },
  {
    front: 'Retention drag',
    label: 'Decision flow without action logic',
    back: 'Users understand the dashboard but not the move. Delay becomes a product problem after looking like low engagement.',
  },
];
---

<section id="cost" class="section-focus">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="section-label">Cost of not acting</p>
    <div class="section-heading-row">
      <h2>The cost is already being paid somewhere.</h2>
      <p>These are decision signals, not promises. The question is whether the recurring cost of not diagnosing the flow is already higher than the review.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-card-row">
      {costs.map((cost) => (
        <button class="metric-reveal-card" type="button" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="metric-front">
            <strong>{cost.front}</strong>
            <em>{cost.label}</em>
          </span>
          <span class="metric-back">{cost.back}</span>
        </button>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.metric-reveal-card').forEach((card) => {
    card.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const expanded = card.getAttribute('aria-expanded') === 'true';
      card.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(!expanded));
    });
  });
</script>
